# core/palette_catalogs.jl — GENERATED from a real Manim CE 0.20.1 — do NOT edit
# by hand.
#
# Manim CE ships six named colour catalogues besides its own palette. They are
# NOT part of `from manim import *` even there: you import the one you want.
# Janim keeps that shape — one submodule each, nothing exported — so 2138
# names cannot collide with the engine's vocabulary by accident:
#
#     julia> Janim.XKCD.MUDDYGREEN     # CE strips the spaces: no underscore
#     "#657432"
#
#     julia> using Janim.DVIPSNAMES        # the whole catalogue, deliberately
#
# The values are Manim CE's, verbatim, and they are not always the values the
# same name has elsewhere: SVGNAMES.ALICEBLUE is "#EFF7FF" (dvips/xcolor's
# svgnames) while X11.ALICEBLUE is "#F0F8FF" (the CSS one). Both are here
# because both are in CE.
#
# Julia already answers for most of X11 and SVGNAMES through Colors.jl —
# `colorant"aliceblue"` works in any Janim scene — so these two are for
# translating a CE scene that spells them CE's way. AS2700, BS381, DVIPSNAMES and
# XKCD have no equivalent in Colors.jl.

"""
    Janim.ManimCE

Manim CE 0.20.1's palette at Manim CE's own values — all 89 of them, including
the three that ManimGL spells differently and that Janim therefore exports
with GL's value: `ManimCE.YELLOW` and `ManimCE.YELLOW_C` are `"#F7D96F"`
where [`YELLOW`](@ref Janim.YELLOW) is `"#FFFF00"`, and `ManimCE.BLUE_E` is
`"#236B8E"` where [`BLUE_E`](@ref Janim.BLUE_E) is `"#1C758A"`.

This is the escape hatch for code that has to match a CE render to the byte:

```julia
using Janim
set_fill!(m; color = Janim.ManimCE.YELLOW)     # CE's soft gold, not GL's pure
```

Everything else in it is identical to the exported palette, so reaching for
this module for any other colour buys nothing.
"""
